"We can use the principle of inclusion-exclusion to find the number of students who did at least one of the homework.\n\nLet $M$ be the set of students who did math homework, $E$ be the set of students who did English homework, and $S$ be the set of students who did science homework.\n\nThen, we have:\n\n$$|M \\cup E \\cup S| = |M| + |E| + |S| - |M \\cap E| - |E \\cap S| - |M \\cap S| - |M \\cap E \\cap S|$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we get:\n\n$$|M \\cup E \\cup S| = 59 + 49 + 42 - 31 - 20 - 29 + 12 = 82$$\n\nTherefore, the number of students who did no math, no English and no science homework is:\n\n$$100 - |M \\cup E \\cup S| = 100 - 82 = \\boxed{8}$$\nFinal Answer: The final answer is $8$. I hope it is correct."
