"Let the smaller number be \\( x \\) and the larger number be \\( y \\).\n\nWe are given two equations:\n1. \\( x + y = 15 \\)\n2. \\( 4x = 2y - 60 \\)\n\nFirst, we solve the second equation for \\( y \\):\n\\[ 4x = 2y - 60 \\]\n\\[ 4x = 2y - 60 \\]\n\\[ 2x = y - 30 \\]\n\\[ y = 30 - 2x \\]\n\nNow, substitute \\( y = 30 - 2x \\) into the first equation:\n\\[ x + (30 - 2x) = 15 \\]\n\\[ x + 30 - 2x = 15 \\]\n\\[ 30 = 15 \\]\n\nThis equation is a contradiction, which means there is no solution for \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) that satisfies both equations simultaneously. Therefore, the problem has no solution.\n\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\boxed{\\text{No solution}}."
