"To determine the probability that all four coins (a penny, a nickel, a dime, and a quarter) come up heads when flipped simultaneously, we need to consider the probability of each coin coming up heads and then multiply these probabilities together.\n\nEach coin has two possible outcomes: heads (H) or tails (T). Therefore, the probability of each coin coming up heads is \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\nSince the flips are independent, the probability of all four coins coming up heads is the product of the probabilities of each individual coin coming up heads:\n\n\\[\n\\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) \\times \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right) = \\left( \\frac{1}{2} \\right)^4 = \\frac{1}{16}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that all four coins come up heads is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{16}}\\).\nFinal Answer: The final answer is \\(\\frac{1}{16}\\). I hope it is correct."
